 **Runtime contract: failure status and the concurrency token.** `Poly.Runtime.DomainFailureStatus.Http(kind)` is the only kind→status table: `Unique`, `Link`, `Conflict` and `Stage` are 409, `Policy` is 422, `Cascade` is 500, the other eight kinds are 400, and an unknown kind throws rather than inheriting 400. A host maps a failure through it — it never parses failure text (the generated host's one `Problem(DomainFailure)` takes the status from the kind and returns ProblemDetails; P-14a deleted the `message.Contains(...)` shim) and never prints a second table (#677 g.10). `Link` → 409 means an occupied to-one link only; `Stage` 409 and `Policy` 422 are owner rulings (#677). `Poly.Runtime.IVersioned { long Version { get; } }` is the optimistic-concurrency token: P-09 binds it and owns the shadow column and the save-time bump, while generated plain C# entities are not thread-safe by contract (#574), so concurrent writes stay the store's concern, not the entity's. ADR: [`docs/decisions/2026-11-12-runtime-contract-package.md`](decisions/2026-11-12-runtime-contract-package.md).
 
